==Finnish==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|fi|non|herra}}, from {{der|fi|osx|hērro}}, from {{der|fi|goh|hērro}}, the comparative form of {{m|goh|hēr||noble, venerable}} ({{cog|de|hehr}}), by analogy with Latin {{m|la|senior||elder}}. Cognates include {{cog|da|herre}}, {{cog|no|herre}}, {{cog|sv|herre}}, {{cog|is|herra}}, {{cog|nl|heer}}, {{cog|de|Herr}}. The Old High German word originally meant "grey, grey-haired", and descends from {{der|fi|gem-pro|*hairaz||grey}}, making it cognate with {{cog|ang|hār}} ({{cog|en|hoar}}), {{cog|non|hárr}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{fi-pronunciation|a=Fi-herra.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# [[Mister]] {{gloss|polite title for an adult man}}
#: {{ux|fi|'''Herra''' Virtanen, voinko puhua kanssanne?|'''Mister''' Virtanen, may I speak with you?}}
# [[sir]] {{gloss|polite form of address to a man, often preceded by {{m|fi|hyvä}}}}
#: {{uxi|fi|Anteeksi, '''herra'''...|Excuse me, '''Sir'''...}}
#: {{uxi|fi|Hyvät '''herrat'''!|'''Gentlemen'''!}}
# [[gentleman]] {{gloss|honourable or sophisticated man}}
#: {{ux|fi|Ovesta astui sisään kolme hienoa '''herraa'''.|Three smart '''gentlemen''' stepped in the door.}}
# [[lord]], [[master]] {{gloss|person having authority over someone or something}}
#: {{uxi|fi|kartanon '''herra'''|'''lord''' of the manor}}
#: {{uxi|fi|Sormusten '''herra'''|'''Lord''' of the Rings}}
#: {{uxi|fi|oman itsensä '''herra'''|'''master''' of oneself}}
#: {{uxi|fi|Kärpästen '''herra'''|'''Lord''' of the flies}}
# {{lb|fi|informal}} a [[member]] of the [[ruling]] or [[upper class]], a person of authority; {{lb|fi|in plural}} the [[ruling]] or [[upper class]], the mighty, people in authority {{q|no exact English translation, definitely not any with the same nuance}}
#: {{coi|fi|'''herrojen''' herkku|delicacy (only) '''for the rich''' (because only they can afford it)}}
#: {{coi|fi|'''herrojen''' metkuja|antics of '''the ruling class'''}}
#: {{coi|fi|elää '''herroiksi'''|to live '''like a lord''' / to live '''like a king'''}}
# {{lb|fi|religion}} {{alternative case form of|fi|Herra||Lord}}

